# Local PDF Redactor

A lightweight browser PDF-redaction prototype for GitHub Pages. PDF processing occurs in the browser. Search hits can be reviewed, automatic redactions removed individually, and manual boxes can be drawn, moved, resized, copied and deleted.

## Important security design
Export rasterises every source page and creates a new image-only PDF with black boxes already painted. This removes the original PDF text and object layer from the exported file, unlike merely drawing rectangles over recoverable text. Always verify the exported file before disclosure.

## Run locally
Because JavaScript modules are used, serve the folder rather than opening `index.html` directly:

```bash
python3 -m http.server 8000
```
Then open `http://localhost:8000`.

## GitHub Pages
1. Create a repository and upload the contents of this folder to the default branch.
2. Open **Settings → Pages**.
3. Under **Build and deployment**, choose **Deploy from a branch**.
4. Select the default branch and `/ (root)`, then save.

## Dependencies and privacy
The supplied lightweight GitHub Pages build loads PDF.js, pdf-lib and Tesseract.js from jsDelivr. PDF data is not uploaded by this code, but the browser must contact the CDN to fetch those libraries. For a strict no-cloud/offline deployment, download approved copies of those libraries into `assets/vendor/`, update the script and worker paths in `index.html` and `js/pdfViewer.js`, and deploy those files with the repository. No telemetry or analytics are included.

## Search behaviour
Text-based PDFs use PDF.js text-item positions. Matches spanning several PDF text items may require separate terms or manual redaction. OCR code is provided as a module foundation for scanned pages; the compact build does not yet merge OCR words into the automatic search index. Manual redaction remains available for scanned pages.

## Shortcuts
- Ctrl/Cmd+Z: undo
- Ctrl/Cmd+Shift+Z: redo
- Ctrl/Cmd+D: copy selected rectangle
- Escape: exit manual mode

## Test
Use `assets/example.pdf`.

